1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,128
Sweetheart, spritz cookies are the little butter

2
00:00:03,128 --> 00:00:05,539
cookies pressed into Christmas trees,

3
00:00:05,539 --> 00:00:07,951
wreaths, and stars that were on every

4
00:00:07,951 --> 00:00:09,450
cookie tray in America.

5
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:11,062
Crisp at the edges,

6
00:00:11,062 --> 00:00:12,645
tender in the middle,

7
00:00:12,645 --> 00:00:14,831
and they melt on your tongue.

8
00:00:15,011 --> 00:00:17,298
But most people who try them give

9
00:00:17,298 --> 00:00:18,753
up after ten minutes,

10
00:00:18,753 --> 00:00:21,179
because the dough won't come out of

11
00:00:21,179 --> 00:00:23,327
the press, or it won't stick to

12
00:00:23,327 --> 00:00:23,881
the pan.

13
00:00:24,061 --> 00:00:27,544
And it's always the same five mistakes.

14
00:00:27,895 --> 00:00:30,397
So tonight I'm giving you the spritz

15
00:00:30,397 --> 00:00:33,178
cookies my mother made every December on

16
00:00:33,178 --> 00:00:34,916
Elm Street, step by step,

17
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:37,418
with the cookie press my father gave

18
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:39,226
her in nineteen fifty-one.

19
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:40,568
And stay with me,

20
00:00:40,568 --> 00:00:43,028
because the last secret is where she

21
00:00:43,028 --> 00:00:45,830
put her cookie sheets before every batch,

22
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:48,427
and it's the reason hers never failed.

23
00:00:48,607 --> 00:00:51,315
You probably have the same thing outside

24
00:00:51,315 --> 00:00:53,007
your back door right now.

25
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:57,122
Spritz cookies came from Germany and Scandinavia,

26
00:00:57,122 --> 00:00:57,583
honey.

27
00:00:57,763 --> 00:01:00,593
The word comes from the German spritzen,

28
00:01:00,593 --> 00:01:02,149
which means to squirt,

29
00:01:02,149 --> 00:01:04,838
because you squirt the dough through a

30
00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:05,263
press.

31
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:08,323
The German and Swedish families who settled

32
00:01:08,323 --> 00:01:11,204
in Ohio and Wisconsin and Minnesota brought

33
00:01:11,204 --> 00:01:13,482
them, and by the nineteen fifties,

34
00:01:13,482 --> 00:01:15,425
a cookie press was one of the

35
00:01:15,425 --> 00:01:18,573
most popular Christmas presents in the country.

36
00:01:18,753 --> 00:01:20,646
Every bride got one.

37
00:01:20,996 --> 00:01:23,381
My father bought my mother hers at

38
00:01:23,381 --> 00:01:26,047
Halle's department store in Cleveland,

39
00:01:26,047 --> 00:01:27,590
in nineteen fifty-one.

40
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:30,594
An aluminum tube with a crank on

41
00:01:30,594 --> 00:01:32,976
the top and a little box of

42
00:01:32,976 --> 00:01:34,741
metal discs, a tree,

43
00:01:34,741 --> 00:01:36,241
a wreath, a star,

44
00:01:36,241 --> 00:01:37,829
a camel, a flower,

45
00:01:37,829 --> 00:01:38,358
a dog.

46
00:01:38,538 --> 00:01:41,504
It cost three dollars and forty cents,

47
00:01:41,504 --> 00:01:42,753
which was a lot.

48
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:45,777
She was so excited she made cookies

49
00:01:45,777 --> 00:01:46,996
the same night,

50
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:49,516
and every single one came out a

51
00:01:49,516 --> 00:01:49,922
blob.

52
00:01:50,102 --> 00:01:52,505
In a minute I'll tell you why.

53
00:01:52,856 --> 00:01:54,551
She cried, honey.

54
00:01:54,731 --> 00:01:56,089
She really did.

55
00:01:56,269 --> 00:01:57,352
And my father,

56
00:01:57,352 --> 00:01:59,673
who never baked a thing in his

57
00:01:59,673 --> 00:02:02,923
life, sat down with the little instruction

58
00:02:02,923 --> 00:02:05,167
booklet and read it out loud,

59
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:07,798
every word, while she tried again.

60
00:02:07,978 --> 00:02:10,133
And by the end of the night,

61
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:13,212
they had two dozen perfect little trees,

62
00:02:13,212 --> 00:02:15,675
and flour all over both of them.

63
00:02:15,855 --> 00:02:18,494
She used that press every Christmas for

64
00:02:18,494 --> 00:02:19,779
thirty-eight years.

65
00:02:19,959 --> 00:02:21,434
I use it now.

66
00:02:21,614 --> 00:02:23,286
It still works.

67
00:02:23,636 --> 00:02:26,449
Those cookie presses were made right here

68
00:02:26,449 --> 00:02:27,479
in the Midwest,

69
00:02:27,479 --> 00:02:29,950
honey, and every company had its own

70
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:31,391
little set of shapes.

71
00:02:31,571 --> 00:02:33,446
Some had a Christmas camel,

72
00:02:33,446 --> 00:02:34,904
some had a butterfly,

73
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:37,127
and one had a little Scottie dog

74
00:02:37,127 --> 00:02:39,141
that every child fought over.

75
00:02:39,321 --> 00:02:42,162
Collectors pay good money for the old

76
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:43,315
boxed sets now.

77
00:02:43,495 --> 00:02:47,102
Ours still has the original instruction booklet,

78
00:02:47,102 --> 00:02:49,807
with my father's pencil notes in the

79
00:02:49,807 --> 00:02:50,333
margin.

80
00:02:50,682 --> 00:02:53,281
Here's what you need for about six

81
00:02:53,281 --> 00:02:54,351
dozen cookies.

82
00:02:54,531 --> 00:02:56,631
One cup of real butter,

83
00:02:56,631 --> 00:02:58,548
two sticks, softened.

84
00:02:58,728 --> 00:03:00,957
Two thirds of a cup of sugar.

85
00:03:01,137 --> 00:03:02,101
One egg.

86
00:03:02,281 --> 00:03:05,114
One teaspoon of vanilla extract.

87
00:03:05,294 --> 00:03:07,914
A half teaspoon of almond extract,

88
00:03:07,914 --> 00:03:12,074
which is the old-fashioned flavor everybody remembers.

89
00:03:12,254 --> 00:03:15,001
Two and a quarter cups of all-purpose

90
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:15,447
flour.

91
00:03:15,627 --> 00:03:17,926
And a half teaspoon of salt.

92
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:19,301
That's all.

93
00:03:19,481 --> 00:03:21,181
No baking powder,

94
00:03:21,181 --> 00:03:21,780
honey.

95
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:24,096
Spritz cookies don't rise.

96
00:03:24,276 --> 00:03:26,192
That's how they hold their shape.

97
00:03:26,542 --> 00:03:30,042
For decorating, you need colored sugar,

98
00:03:30,042 --> 00:03:31,298
red and green,

99
00:03:31,298 --> 00:03:33,811
and little cinnamon candies,

100
00:03:33,811 --> 00:03:35,157
and nonpareils.

101
00:03:35,337 --> 00:03:37,639
And if you want green trees and

102
00:03:37,639 --> 00:03:40,089
pink wreaths, a few drops of food

103
00:03:40,089 --> 00:03:41,723
coloring in the dough.

104
00:03:41,903 --> 00:03:43,085
And of course,

105
00:03:43,085 --> 00:03:44,352
a cookie press.

106
00:03:44,532 --> 00:03:46,733
You can find the old metal ones

107
00:03:46,733 --> 00:03:48,791
at any thrift store for a few

108
00:03:48,791 --> 00:03:51,559
dollars, and they work better than most

109
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:53,263
of the new plastic ones.

110
00:03:53,443 --> 00:03:55,277
Or ask your grandmother.

111
00:03:55,457 --> 00:03:58,174
She's got one in a drawer somewhere.

112
00:03:58,524 --> 00:04:01,557
Mistake number one is butter that's too

113
00:04:01,557 --> 00:04:02,878
soft or too hard.

114
00:04:03,058 --> 00:04:04,983
If the butter's melted,

115
00:04:04,983 --> 00:04:07,493
the cookies spread into blobs.

116
00:04:07,673 --> 00:04:08,672
If it's cold,

117
00:04:08,672 --> 00:04:10,976
the dough is too stiff to come

118
00:04:10,976 --> 00:04:12,282
out of the press.

119
00:04:12,462 --> 00:04:14,611
Leave it on the counter about an

120
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:16,760
hour, until you can press a dent

121
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:18,305
in it with your finger,

122
00:04:18,305 --> 00:04:20,253
but it still holds its shape.

123
00:04:20,433 --> 00:04:23,069
Beat the butter and sugar together for

124
00:04:23,069 --> 00:04:25,982
three minutes, until it's pale and fluffy.

125
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:29,355
If Grandma's recipe is helping you,

126
00:04:29,355 --> 00:04:31,687
tap the like button for me,

127
00:04:31,687 --> 00:04:32,637
sweetheart.

128
00:04:32,817 --> 00:04:36,056
It helps more people find these videos.

129
00:04:36,236 --> 00:04:39,429
Mistake number two is too much flour.

130
00:04:39,609 --> 00:04:41,397
Measure it the right way.

131
00:04:41,577 --> 00:04:44,234
Spoon the flour into your measuring cup

132
00:04:44,234 --> 00:04:46,279
and level it off with a knife.

133
00:04:46,459 --> 00:04:48,815
Don't scoop the cup into the bag,

134
00:04:48,815 --> 00:04:50,599
because that packs it in,

135
00:04:50,599 --> 00:04:52,384
and you end up with a dry

136
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:54,597
dough that cracks in the press.

137
00:04:54,777 --> 00:04:57,612
Then beat in the egg and extracts,

138
00:04:57,612 --> 00:05:00,197
and stir in the flour and salt,

139
00:05:00,197 --> 00:05:02,115
just until it's smooth.

140
00:05:02,464 --> 00:05:06,091
And here's something most recipes get wrong,

141
00:05:06,091 --> 00:05:06,586
honey.

142
00:05:06,766 --> 00:05:08,484
Don't chill spritz dough.

143
00:05:08,664 --> 00:05:10,538
Every other cookie dough,

144
00:05:10,538 --> 00:05:11,288
you chill.

145
00:05:11,468 --> 00:05:12,885
Not this one.

146
00:05:13,065 --> 00:05:15,962
Cold spritz dough won't go through the

147
00:05:15,962 --> 00:05:16,420
press.

148
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:18,903
You want it soft and pliable,

149
00:05:18,903 --> 00:05:20,570
about like play clay.

150
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:23,402
Pack it into the press right away,

151
00:05:23,402 --> 00:05:26,288
while it's still at room temperature.

152
00:05:26,468 --> 00:05:28,334
In a minute I'll tell you the

153
00:05:28,334 --> 00:05:30,265
one thing that should be cold.

154
00:05:30,615 --> 00:05:33,866
Mistake number three is greasing the pan,

155
00:05:33,866 --> 00:05:36,880
or using parchment paper or a silicone

156
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:37,197
mat.

157
00:05:37,377 --> 00:05:40,826
That's why most people's cookies won't stick.

158
00:05:41,006 --> 00:05:43,524
The way a cookie press works is,

159
00:05:43,524 --> 00:05:45,886
the dough has to grab onto the

160
00:05:45,886 --> 00:05:48,404
pan, so when you lift the press,

161
00:05:48,404 --> 00:05:50,294
the cookie stays behind.

162
00:05:50,474 --> 00:05:52,907
On a greased pan or parchment,

163
00:05:52,907 --> 00:05:55,502
it just lifts right back up with

164
00:05:55,502 --> 00:05:56,313
the press.

165
00:05:56,493 --> 00:05:57,531
Use a plain,

166
00:05:57,531 --> 00:06:00,905
ungreased, uncoated metal cookie sheet.

167
00:06:01,085 --> 00:06:02,873
Nothing on it at all.

168
00:06:03,222 --> 00:06:05,714
Hold the press straight up and down,

169
00:06:05,714 --> 00:06:08,135
with the bottom resting flat on the

170
00:06:08,135 --> 00:06:08,412
pan.

171
00:06:08,592 --> 00:06:10,926
Give the crank or the trigger one

172
00:06:10,926 --> 00:06:11,704
full click.

173
00:06:11,884 --> 00:06:14,008
Then lift the press straight up.

174
00:06:14,188 --> 00:06:15,314
Don't tilt it,

175
00:06:15,314 --> 00:06:16,441
don't drag it.

176
00:06:16,621 --> 00:06:18,352
One click, lift,

177
00:06:18,352 --> 00:06:20,300
move over an inch,

178
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:22,031
one click, lift.

179
00:06:22,211 --> 00:06:24,521
Your first two or three will be

180
00:06:24,521 --> 00:06:25,415
ugly, honey.

181
00:06:25,595 --> 00:06:27,104
Everybody's are.

182
00:06:27,284 --> 00:06:29,501
Scrape them off and put the dough

183
00:06:29,501 --> 00:06:30,709
back in the press.

184
00:06:30,889 --> 00:06:31,822
After a few,

185
00:06:31,822 --> 00:06:33,687
you'll find your rhythm.

186
00:06:34,038 --> 00:06:36,323
If the dough won't come out at

187
00:06:36,323 --> 00:06:38,380
all, honey, it's too stiff.

188
00:06:38,560 --> 00:06:40,031
Take the dough out,

189
00:06:40,031 --> 00:06:42,432
work in a teaspoon of milk with

190
00:06:42,432 --> 00:06:44,446
your hands, and try again.

191
00:06:44,626 --> 00:06:47,144
If the cookies come out but spread

192
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:48,699
and lose their lines,

193
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:50,773
the dough is too soft or the

194
00:06:50,773 --> 00:06:52,254
kitchen is too warm.

195
00:06:52,434 --> 00:06:54,790
Work in a tablespoon of flour.

196
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:57,664
Don't give up after the first try.

197
00:06:57,844 --> 00:07:00,479
Even my mother cried the first night.

198
00:07:00,659 --> 00:07:02,821
I'll tell you how the cookie sheet

199
00:07:02,821 --> 00:07:04,793
fixes most of this in a minute.

200
00:07:05,142 --> 00:07:07,388
So far, butter with a dent,

201
00:07:07,388 --> 00:07:08,718
spoon and level,

202
00:07:08,718 --> 00:07:10,548
don't chill the dough,

203
00:07:10,548 --> 00:07:12,294
and a bare metal pan.

204
00:07:12,474 --> 00:07:15,298
Mistake number four is putting the next

205
00:07:15,298 --> 00:07:16,747
batch on a warm pan.

206
00:07:16,927 --> 00:07:19,006
The butter in the dough melts the

207
00:07:19,006 --> 00:07:20,958
second it touches a warm sheet,

208
00:07:20,958 --> 00:07:23,163
and the cookie won't hold its shape

209
00:07:23,163 --> 00:07:23,730
or stick.

210
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:26,411
Always let your pans cool all the

211
00:07:26,411 --> 00:07:27,927
way between batches.

212
00:07:28,107 --> 00:07:30,858
Use two or three pans and rotate

213
00:07:30,858 --> 00:07:31,288
them.

214
00:07:31,468 --> 00:07:32,360
Stay with me,

215
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:35,102
because this is connected to the secret.

216
00:07:35,453 --> 00:07:38,630
Sprinkle the colored sugar on right away,

217
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:39,948
before they bake,

218
00:07:39,948 --> 00:07:40,956
so it sticks.

219
00:07:41,136 --> 00:07:44,251
Then bake at three hundred seventy-five degrees

220
00:07:44,251 --> 00:07:45,907
for six to eight minutes.

221
00:07:46,087 --> 00:07:48,630
Mistake number five is overbaking.

222
00:07:48,810 --> 00:07:51,504
Spritz cookies should be pale on top,

223
00:07:51,504 --> 00:07:54,197
with just the faintest golden edge on

224
00:07:54,197 --> 00:07:54,998
the bottom.

225
00:07:55,178 --> 00:07:56,431
If they're brown,

226
00:07:56,431 --> 00:07:58,348
they taste like cardboard.

227
00:07:58,528 --> 00:08:00,563
Let them sit on the pan for

228
00:08:00,563 --> 00:08:02,900
one minute, then move them to a

229
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,785
rack with a thin spatula.

230
00:08:05,136 --> 00:08:07,102
Here's my mother's secret,

231
00:08:07,102 --> 00:08:07,934
sweetheart.

232
00:08:08,114 --> 00:08:10,036
Before every single batch,

233
00:08:10,036 --> 00:08:12,625
she set her empty cookie sheets out

234
00:08:12,625 --> 00:08:13,956
on the back porch,

235
00:08:13,956 --> 00:08:14,844
in the snow,

236
00:08:14,844 --> 00:08:16,101
for five minutes.

237
00:08:16,281 --> 00:08:18,263
Ohio in December,

238
00:08:18,263 --> 00:08:18,963
honey.

239
00:08:19,143 --> 00:08:21,314
The porch was a refrigerator.

240
00:08:21,494 --> 00:08:24,171
And she pressed the cookies onto those

241
00:08:24,171 --> 00:08:25,720
ice-cold metal sheets.

242
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:28,369
The dough grabs onto cold metal like

243
00:08:28,369 --> 00:08:29,534
it's glued there.

244
00:08:29,714 --> 00:08:31,456
Every cookie sticks.

245
00:08:31,636 --> 00:08:33,772
Every edge stays sharp.

246
00:08:33,952 --> 00:08:35,531
Not one blob.

247
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,520
If you don't have snow,

248
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,944
put your bare sheet in the freezer

249
00:08:39,944 --> 00:08:41,084
for ten minutes,

250
00:08:41,084 --> 00:08:43,508
or in the refrigerator for twenty.

251
00:08:43,688 --> 00:08:44,871
Just the sheet,

252
00:08:44,871 --> 00:08:46,684
honey, never the dough.

253
00:08:46,864 --> 00:08:49,186
Cold pan, soft dough.

254
00:08:49,366 --> 00:08:50,991
That's the whole trick.

255
00:08:51,171 --> 00:08:53,415
My father figured it out from the

256
00:08:53,415 --> 00:08:55,931
instruction booklet that first night.

257
00:08:56,111 --> 00:08:59,025
It said, use cool baking sheets.

258
00:08:59,205 --> 00:09:00,875
And my mother said,

259
00:09:00,875 --> 00:09:02,984
Walter, if cool is good,

260
00:09:02,984 --> 00:09:04,302
cold is better.

261
00:09:04,482 --> 00:09:05,910
And she was right.

262
00:09:06,259 --> 00:09:07,452
For green trees,

263
00:09:07,452 --> 00:09:09,912
mix a few drops of green coloring

264
00:09:09,912 --> 00:09:11,403
into half the dough.

265
00:09:11,583 --> 00:09:14,414
Put a single cinnamon candy on top

266
00:09:14,414 --> 00:09:15,413
of each one,

267
00:09:15,413 --> 00:09:16,412
like a star.

268
00:09:16,592 --> 00:09:18,851
For wreaths, use plain dough,

269
00:09:18,851 --> 00:09:21,032
dip the tops in green sugar,

270
00:09:21,032 --> 00:09:23,602
and put three tiny red candies at

271
00:09:23,602 --> 00:09:25,393
the bottom for berries.

272
00:09:25,573 --> 00:09:27,673
You can also dip half of each

273
00:09:27,673 --> 00:09:30,641
cooled cookie in melted chocolate and lay

274
00:09:30,641 --> 00:09:32,307
it on wax paper to set.

275
00:09:32,487 --> 00:09:34,623
Children love doing that part.

276
00:09:34,973 --> 00:09:37,007
They keep for two weeks in a

277
00:09:37,007 --> 00:09:39,768
tin, honey, with wax paper between the

278
00:09:39,768 --> 00:09:42,893
layers, and they freeze beautifully for two

279
00:09:42,893 --> 00:09:43,401
months.

280
00:09:43,581 --> 00:09:46,437
My mother made twelve dozen every year.

281
00:09:46,617 --> 00:09:49,212
She packed them in coffee cans with

282
00:09:49,212 --> 00:09:52,251
Christmas paper glued around the outside,

283
00:09:52,251 --> 00:09:54,401
and gave them to the mailman,

284
00:09:54,401 --> 00:09:56,329
the milkman, the minister,

285
00:09:56,329 --> 00:09:58,552
and every widow on our street.

286
00:09:58,732 --> 00:10:01,296
Danny and I delivered them on our

287
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:01,763
